1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,784
On Sunday evening, Aziz wants to save a pair of running shoes in the marketplace, on his laptop.

2
00:00:05,834 --> 00:00:07,776
The web page asks for his phone number.

3
00:00:07,826 --> 00:00:09,648
Nothing more.

4
00:00:09,698 --> 00:00:16,664
So what happens between that phone number and a customer who can use every app of the bank?

5
00:00:16,714 --> 00:00:26,024
In Lesson 9, the apps learned to call the heeyID API through its contract, and only the master app got the MyID and TAF parts.

6
00:00:26,074 --> 00:00:32,408
This lesson follows the first call of that contract: the registration, from a phone number to level one.

7
00:00:32,458 --> 00:00:35,240
Aziz types his number and taps Continue.

8
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:41,504
The marketplace sends the app, the number, the language of the journey and a short description of the device.

9
00:00:41,554 --> 00:00:44,312
The request also carries its network address.

10
00:00:44,362 --> 00:00:46,832
The platform checks three facts.

11
00:00:46,882 --> 00:00:49,880
The number is a real mobile number of Uzbekistan.

12
00:00:49,930 --> 00:00:51,752
The app is active.

13
00:00:51,802 --> 00:00:56,024
And no limit is reached, which is the subject of Lesson 11.

14
00:00:56,074 --> 00:01:00,104
Then the platform opens a registration record, in the state Started.

15
00:01:00,154 --> 00:01:05,912
From now on, every text and every code message to Aziz is in his language.

16
00:01:05,962 --> 00:01:08,768
Next, the platform makes a one-time code.

17
00:01:08,818 --> 00:01:10,640
The code is random.

18
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:14,048
It never comes from the number, the time or the device.

19
00:01:14,098 --> 00:01:19,640
The platform keeps it only as a digest, so even the database cannot say the code back.

20
00:01:19,690 --> 00:01:24,440
The marketplace gets the lifetime of the code and the number of resends that remain.

21
00:01:24,490 --> 00:01:26,312
It never gets the code.

22
00:01:26,362 --> 00:01:28,256
Who sends the SMS?

23
00:01:28,306 --> 00:01:30,128
Here the app decides.

24
00:01:30,178 --> 00:01:35,480
The marketplace web profile is a side app, and side apps do not contain the TAF part.

25
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:41,264
The TAF check works only when TAF itself sends the code to the phone that types it.

26
00:01:41,314 --> 00:01:47,336
So in a side app, on a phone or on the web, the bank's own SMS service always sends the code.

27
00:01:47,386 --> 00:01:50,816
Aziz reads the code on his phone and types it on his laptop.

28
00:01:50,866 --> 00:01:53,264
The platform compares it with the digest.

29
00:01:53,314 --> 00:01:57,824
They match, so the record moves to Number verified, and the code is void.

30
00:01:57,874 --> 00:01:59,696
There is no verdict on the web.

31
00:01:59,746 --> 00:02:02,912
The audit event simply says that the source was web.

32
00:02:02,962 --> 00:02:06,896
Now one rule that I think every identity team should copy.

33
00:02:06,946 --> 00:02:14,336
Until the number is verified, the platform makes nothing for it: no consent, no device record, no session.

34
00:02:14,386 --> 00:02:18,608
A request that tries gets one answer: number not verified.

35
00:02:18,658 --> 00:02:20,816
Next, the platform looks up the number.

36
00:02:20,866 --> 00:02:24,176
It belongs to nobody, so this is a new person.

37
00:02:24,226 --> 00:02:29,648
The marketplace asks Aziz for a password, and then for the same password a second time.

38
00:02:29,698 --> 00:02:36,008
Aziz types two different words by mistake, and the platform refuses: the two entries are not the same.

39
00:02:36,058 --> 00:02:37,880
He tries again.

40
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:45,512
This time the password is long enough, it is not on the list of common and breached passwords, and it does not contain his phone number.

41
00:02:45,562 --> 00:02:48,848
Nobody asks him for a symbol or a capital letter.

42
00:02:48,898 --> 00:02:51,776
Lesson 13 shows how the platform keeps it.

43
00:02:51,826 --> 00:02:53,744
Now four things appear at once.

44
00:02:53,794 --> 00:02:55,616
A customer at level one.

45
00:02:55,666 --> 00:02:57,656
An app account in the marketplace.

46
00:02:57,706 --> 00:03:01,016
A device record for the browser, which can never become trusted.

47
00:03:01,066 --> 00:03:02,888
And a session for this browser.

48
00:03:02,938 --> 00:03:05,528
The registration record is now Onboarded.

49
00:03:05,578 --> 00:03:11,480
The marketplace needs only level one to keep a wish list, so Aziz uses it at once.

50
00:03:11,530 --> 00:03:17,096
On Monday morning, Aziz installs the mobile bank on his phone, and types the same number.

51
00:03:17,146 --> 00:03:19,376
This time the code goes another way.

52
00:03:19,426 --> 00:03:24,320
The mobile bank is the master app, so the platform sends the code through TAF.

53
00:03:24,370 --> 00:03:31,304
TAF sends the SMS, and its part inside the app checks that the code arrived on this phone, with this SIM card.

54
00:03:31,354 --> 00:03:38,816
If TAF does not answer, the bank's SMS service sends the code instead, and Lesson 12 shows what that costs.

55
00:03:38,866 --> 00:03:42,296
Aziz types the code, and the number is verified again.

56
00:03:42,346 --> 00:03:47,168
But now the number belongs to a customer: Aziz himself, from Sunday.

57
00:03:47,218 --> 00:03:49,256
So this is not a registration.

58
00:03:49,306 --> 00:03:52,184
The platform does not show the existing account.

59
00:03:52,234 --> 00:03:55,136
It asks for the same password, the one from Sunday.

60
00:03:55,186 --> 00:04:00,032
Then it continues as a login from a new device, and it adds an app account for the mobile bank.

61
00:04:00,082 --> 00:04:03,416
Still one person, one number, one password.

62
00:04:03,466 --> 00:04:06,608
What if the person on Monday did not know that password?

63
00:04:06,658 --> 00:04:10,856
Then the only way forward is a password reset, never a second account.

64
00:04:10,906 --> 00:04:17,168
And if the number belonged to a blocked customer, the journey stops with one sentence: Please contact the bank.

65
00:04:17,218 --> 00:04:19,040
One more case.

66
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:23,768
A registration that never reaches level one does not wait forever.

67
00:04:23,818 --> 00:04:26,960
After the configured time, the record expires.

68
00:04:27,010 --> 00:04:30,920
The platform deletes its data and keeps only the audit events.

69
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:32,816
A new try starts from the beginning.

70
00:04:32,866 --> 00:04:34,688
To recap.

71
00:04:34,738 --> 00:04:36,560
The app sends the number.

72
00:04:36,610 --> 00:04:41,936
The platform opens a record, and makes a random code that it keeps only as a digest.

73
00:04:41,986 --> 00:04:48,776
The master app gets its code through TAF, and every side app through the bank's SMS service.

74
00:04:48,826 --> 00:04:55,496
A verified number then becomes a new customer at level one, or a login for a customer who already exists.

75
00:04:55,546 --> 00:04:57,368
But look at Sunday evening again.

76
00:04:57,418 --> 00:04:59,912
The code went to a number that anyone can type.

77
00:04:59,962 --> 00:05:04,088
What stops a stranger from asking for codes all night, or from guessing one?

78
00:05:04,138 --> 00:05:06,368
Next: codes and limits.
